description:
Sites:
- crate-jmap-jscalendar-types/src/lib.rs:42   pub struct LocalDateTime(pub String);
- crate-jmap-jscalendar-types/src/lib.rs:66   pub struct Duration(pub String);
- crate-jmap-jscalendar-types/src/lib.rs:91   pub struct SignedDuration(pub String);
- crate-jmap-jscontact-types/src/lib.rs:62    pub struct JsContactId(pub String);

Each of these crates' AGENTS.md declares a settled design constraint:

> All public structs: #[non_exhaustive]

(See crate-jmap-jscalendar-types/AGENTS.md "Design constraints (settled)" and the parallel section in crate-jmap-jscontact-types/AGENTS.md.)

Every named-field public struct in both crates correctly carries `#[non_exhaustive]` — the violation is restricted to the 4 tuple newtypes above.

Workspace precedent (`jmap-types`) has these as `#[non_exhaustive] pub struct Foo(String)`:
- crate-jmap-types/src/id.rs:59-60   #[non_exhaustive] pub struct Id(String);
- crate-jmap-types/src/id.rs:66-67   #[non_exhaustive] pub struct State(String);
- crate-jmap-types/src/id.rs       #[non_exhaustive] pub struct UTCDate(String);

i.e. the precedent applies `#[non_exhaustive]` even to tuple newtypes with private inner fields. The non_exhaustive on a unit/tuple struct prevents pattern-matching with destructuring outside the crate (`let Foo(s) = foo` becomes a hard error), forcing all access through accessor methods. That's the lock the design constraint is buying.

The fix is one-line per type (add `#[non_exhaustive]` above each `pub struct` line). Couples with bead .72 (above) to keep the public-field problem and the non_exhaustive problem fixed together.

Why P2: SemVer-affecting if/when these crates publish to crates.io. Adding `#[non_exhaustive]` later is itself a breaking change. The crates haven't published yet; fix now is cheap, fix later is expensive.
